1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:01,893
Meet wreck it.

2
00:00:01,894 --> 00:00:06,494
It turns your coding agent into a QA team for
your local web app.

3
00:00:07,152 --> 00:00:12,012
Start your app locally, install the skills, and
set up the browser tools.

4
00:00:12,013 --> 00:00:16,407
Your agent uses them to explore the app and
record what breaks.

5
00:00:17,060 --> 00:00:21,559
Ask it to test your app, or focus on one flow,
like checkout.

6
00:00:21,560 --> 00:00:27,527
The agent walks through the flow, checks edge
cases, and reproduces the problems it finds.

7
00:00:28,192 --> 00:00:30,719
Here is a seeded demo bug.

8
00:00:30,720 --> 00:00:36,145
Three pairs of earbuds should total two hundred
thirty-nine dollars and ninety-seven cents.

9
00:00:36,146 --> 00:00:39,599
Checkout displays just seventy-nine ninety-nine.

10
00:00:40,260 --> 00:00:45,880
The finding includes reproduction steps,
relevant screenshots, and the source location.

11
00:00:45,881 --> 00:00:50,855
Here, the subtotal adds each unit price once,
ignoring the quantity.

12
00:00:51,512 --> 00:00:55,470
Open the report and review the generated
Playwright test.

13
00:00:55,471 --> 00:01:01,437
This check fails on the buggy copy, then passes
after the subtotal calculation is corrected.

14
00:01:02,092 --> 00:01:04,980
Try wreck it on your next local build.

15
00:01:04,981 --> 00:01:07,881
Wreck your app before your users do.
